## 1. Aim

To understand shallow and deep copying by implementing a copy constructor, copy assignment operator and destructor for a Prescription class.

## 2. Shallow Copy – Without Copy Constructor

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
using namespace std;

class Prescription {
    int n;
    string* medicines;

public:
    Prescription(int count) {
        n = count;
        medicines = new string[n];
    }

    ~Prescription() {
        delete[] medicines;
    }

    void set(int i, string m) {
        medicines[i] = m;
    }

    void display() {
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++)
            cout << medicines[i] << " ";
        cout << endl;
    }
};

int main() {
    Prescription p(2);
    p.set(0, "Paracetamol");
    p.set(1, "ORS");

    Prescription q = p;

    q.set(1, "Cetirizine");

    p.display();
    q.display();

    return 0;
}
```

**Observation:**

- The compiler-generated copy constructor copies only the pointer.
- Both objects point to the same memory.
- Changing one object affects the other.
- When both objects are destroyed, the same memory is deleted twice, causing a double-free error.

Run this version using:

```bash
g++ -std=c++17 -fsanitize=address task2.cpp -o task2
```

## 3. Deep Copy – Correct Program

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
using namespace std;

class Prescription {
    int n;
    string* medicines;

public:
    Prescription(int count) : n(count), medicines(new string[count]) {
    }

    // Copy constructor
    Prescription(const Prescription& p)
        : n(p.n), medicines(new string[p.n]) {
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++)
            medicines[i] = p.medicines[i];

        cout << "Copy constructor called" << endl;
    }

    // Copy assignment operator
    Prescription& operator=(const Prescription& p) {
        if (this != &p) {
            string* temp = new string[p.n];

            for (int i = 0; i < p.n; i++)
                temp[i] = p.medicines[i];

            delete[] medicines;
            medicines = temp;
            n = p.n;
        }
        return *this;
    }

    // Destructor
    ~Prescription() {
        delete[] medicines;
    }

    void set(int i, const string& m) {
        medicines[i] = m;
    }

    string get(int i) const {
        return medicines[i];
    }
};

int main() {
    Prescription p(2);

    p.set(0, "Paracetamol");
    p.set(1, "ORS");

    Prescription q = p;
    q.set(1, "Cetirizine");

    Prescription r(1);
    r = p;

    p.set(0, "Ibuprofen");

    cout << p.get(0) << " " << p.get(1) << " | ";
    cout << q.get(0) << " " << q.get(1) << " | ";
    cout << r.get(0) << " " << r.get(1) << endl;

    return 0;
}
```

## 4. Output

```text
Copy constructor called
Ibuprofen ORS | Paracetamol Cetirizine | Paracetamol ORS
```

**Result:** Each object has its own memory. Changing one prescription does not affect the others.

## 5. Design and Analysis

**A. Memory diagrams**

Shallow copy:

```text
p ─────┐
       ├──> [Paracetamol] [ORS]
q ─────┘
```

Deep copy:

```text
p ─────> [Paracetamol] [ORS]

q ─────> [Paracetamol] [ORS]
```

Each object has a separate array in deep copying.

**B. Why does shallow copying crash?**

Both objects share the same pointer. When the first object is destroyed, its memory is deleted. The second object tries to delete the same memory again, causing a double-free error.

**C. Rule of Three**

The Rule of Three states that if a class needs one of these three functions, it usually needs all three:

1. Copy constructor
2. Copy assignment operator
3. Destructor

They are required to manage dynamically allocated memory safely.

**D. Three situations where a copy constructor is called**

1. When one object is initialized using another object.
2. When an object is passed to a function by value.
3. When an object is returned from a function by value (if copying is not elided).

## 6. Experimental Investigation

**A. Pass by value and const reference**

```cpp
void byValue(Prescription p) {
    cout << "Passed by value" << endl;
}

void byReference(const Prescription& p) {
    cout << "Passed by reference" << endl;
}
```

- Pass by value creates a copy and calls the copy constructor.
- Pass by const reference does not create a copy.

**B. Self-assignment test**

```cpp
p = p;
```

The condition `if (this != &p)` prevents unnecessary copying and memory errors.

**C. Measuring copy time**

Test prescriptions containing:

- 10 medicines
- 1,000 medicines
- 100,000 medicines

Use `chrono` to measure the time taken for copying. Deep copying takes more time as the number of medicines increases.

**D. Sanitizer test**

Compile using:

```bash
g++ -std=c++17 -fsanitize=address -g task2.cpp -o task2
```

The shallow-copy version reports a double-free error. The deep-copy version should run without memory errors.

