## 8. Alternative Concept – Using vector

Instead of a dynamically allocated array, we can use `vector<string>`.

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <vector>
using namespace std;

class Prescription {
    vector<string> medicines;

public:
    Prescription(int n) : medicines(n) {
    }
};
```

A vector automatically manages memory and performs deep copying. Therefore, we do not need to write a copy constructor, copy assignment operator or destructor.

**Complexity comparison:**

| Method | Time | Space |
|---|---|---|
| Shallow copy | O(1) | O(1) |
| Deep copy | O(n) | O(n) |
| Vector copy | O(n) | O(n) |

Deep copying and vector copying create independent copies. Shallow copying only copies the pointer.

## 9. Conclusion

Deep copying ensures that each Prescription object has independent memory. The Rule of Three helps prevent memory errors. Using vector is simpler because it manages memory automatically.
